## 3. Test Users

The following five user accounts must be created in the test database before any test runs. They must have fixed, known UUIDs so tests can reference them reliably. Passwords must be stored as bcrypt hashes — the values below are the plaintext passwords the tests will submit.

| UUID | Email | Password (plaintext) | Role |
|---|---|---|---|
| `aaaaaaaa-0001-0001-0001-000000000001` | admin@test.com | AdminPass123! | ADMIN |
| `bbbbbbbb-0002-0002-0002-000000000002` | sellera@test.com | SellerPass123! | SELLER |
| `cccccccc-0003-0003-0003-000000000003` | sellerb@test.com | SellerPass123! | SELLER |
| `dddddddd-0004-0004-0004-000000000004` | buyera@test.com | BuyerPass123! | BUYER |
| `eeeeeeee-0005-0005-0005-000000000005` | buyerb@test.com | BuyerPass123! | BUYER |

**Why two sellers and two buyers?** The IDOR tests require proving that Seller A cannot access Seller B's resources, and Buyer A cannot access Buyer B's orders. You need two separate accounts per role to test cross-user isolation.

**Why fixed UUIDs?** If UUIDs are randomly generated at seed time, your tests cannot know them in advance. Fixed UUIDs let you hardcode references in test fixtures without querying the database first.

